# Tracker review lenses

### 3. Run Reviews

> **Inline dispatch.** Each sub-review below runs **inline in this wrapper's context** — invoke each skill directly (`@skills/<name>/SKILL.md` with any `MODE=cr` flag), passing the PR URL / number and the branch already checked out, and declare the publishing contract for this CR run (quiet vs publish; see step 4). Each invoked skill must return its findings as the canonical markdown block (`## Assignment Compliance` block, Critical / Moderate lists with reproducer fields). The CR wrapper then assembles the outputs into the final GitHub PR comment plus the non-technical tracker summary. Run the sub-reviews **one at a time** — do not dispatch them as parallel subagents.
>
> The conditional lenses follow the same rule: when their trigger fires, invoke them inline after the always-run set, still one at a time.

- Always run (inline, one at a time; `security-review` may run in parallel, see its entry):
    - `@skills/assignment-compliance-check/SKILL.md` — non-technical business-logic vs assignment check. The skill **does not publish anywhere itself** — it returns the assembled `## Assignment Compliance` markdown block on every run that has a linked tracker, carrying exactly one of three verdicts (every acceptance criterion met / a named criterion not met and what is missing / the assignment states no explicit criteria plus the basis the state was judged on), or the status `no linked issue — assignment compliance skipped` when no linked tracker exists.
The CR wrapper passes the returned block as an embedded block to `@skills/pr-summary/SKILL.md`, so each tracker receives **one consolidated comment** per CR run that always states whether the assignment was met — on the skip status there is no tracker comment at all and the wrapper surfaces the status on the GitHub PR comment summary line. **Do not embed** the block into the GitHub PR comment — keep the PR comment focused on technical findings and surface the consolidated-comment status in the summary line.
    - `@skills/code-review/SKILL.md`
    - `@skills/analyze-problem/SKILL.md` — **always run, scoped to assignment conformance**, invoked inline and read-only (analysis-only — no plan artifact, no code / git writes). Compares the loaded assignment's requirements / acceptance criteria / expected behavior against what the PR diff actually implements and raises every unmet requirement as a **Critical** finding with reproducer fields on the GitHub PR comment. Canonical definition lives in `@skills/code-review/SKILL.md` Specialized Reviews → Always run; it is distinct from the per-Critical-finding verification and must not duplicate gaps already raised by `assignment-compliance-check`.
    - `@skills/security-review/SKILL.md` — **skipped when `SECURITY_OWNER=leonardo` is set by the caller.** When a **separate, concurrent** agent owns the security review, this inline pass repeats the same full review over the same diff and lands the same findings twice; the duplicate is added latency, not added coverage. **Absence of the flag means it runs** — coverage never drops because someone forgot to set it, and a run with no concurrent security pass is unchanged. **No agent this package ships sets the flag**: `leonardo` is the single reviewer, so this inline pass *is* her security pass and suppressing it would skip the review with nothing behind it.
The flag is for an external caller that genuinely runs a concurrent pass. When it is skipped, say so on the summary line (`security: owned by leonardo`) so the PR comment never implies a security pass that did not happen here.
The one exception to *one at a time*: after the `prepare-issue-context` pre-flight has returned, `security-review` may run in parallel with the remaining always-run lenses, as parallel tool calls in this same context, never as a subagent. It still runs exactly once over the current diff, and its findings land in the one review comment of this run.

- Run conditionally:
    - **Database operations detected in the diff → the engine decides where the query analysis runs.** The trigger pattern list and the engine branch are owned by `@skills/code-review/SKILL.md` Specialized Reviews: the patterns (raw SQL, Eloquent / query-builder calls, eager loads, model scopes, ModelManager / Repository methods, migrations, seeders) decide whether the analysis runs, and the resolved engine decides where — `@skills/postgres-patterns/SKILL.md` with `MODE=cr` inline on PostgreSQL; on MySQL / MariaDB and on an unresolved engine the advisory `casey` report (*Database report — the `casey` agent*), never `mysql-problem-solver` inline. NoSQL access fires its own trigger, independent of the engine, and the PostgreSQL query plan is a `casey` part too (same section).
When this wrapper runs in a top-level session, that session dispatches `casey` once, after the final run of the review loop; under `krang`, `casey` runs in every *Parallel CR round* (`commands/_shared/krang-workflow.inc`), and a review inside a subagent still reports the need in its handoff (`agents/leonardo.md` *Output* → `Database report:`). A top-level caller that cannot dispatch it writes `Database report not run — dispatch casey` in its output.
On PostgreSQL, capture the lens's findings and surface them on the GitHub PR comment under the dedicated `## Database Analysis` section (see Output Rules) — never silently fold them into the Critical / Moderate buckets. On MySQL / MariaDB a second, independent trigger — a migration, an upsert, a JSON column, a full-text index, a partition definition, deadlock handling, or read/write splitting — runs `@skills/mysql-patterns/SKILL.md` with `MODE=cr` inline; its findings land in that same single section, never a second one. The non-technical tracker comment **does not** carry this section (it stays plain-language via `pr-summary`).
    - Third-party API or service changes → ensure the **Third-Party API & Service Analysis** step from `@skills/code-review/SKILL.md` is executed for the diff, including the ordered documentation source walk (step 2) and, when it resolves no reference, the blocking documentation request (step 7). Capture every request and publish it on the GitHub PR comment under `## Documentation Requests` (see Output Rules) — the PR comment is the surface the change's author reads, so a request that never reaches it leaves the accompanying Moderate finding unanswerable.

#### Reuse-first gate (PR diff only)

1. Restrict the analysis to lines added or modified in the PR — never review untouched code.
2. Apply the **reuse-first gate** from `@rules/code-review/general.md` *Reuse Existing Logic* to every block of new or modified logic on the diff: first verify the new logic is necessary to satisfy the assignment at all (an existing helper / Service / Action / Data Builder / DTO / trait / ModelManager / Repository / scope may already fulfil it), then, when some logic is genuinely needed, check whether an equivalent implementation already exists to reuse. Either way the finding is a rule violation in the standard severity buckets, at the severity `@rules/code-review/core-analysis.md` *Reuse of existing logic* declares.

